### Pain point 4.3

**Description:** The incremental cost of adding HbA1c to a corporate MCU panel is small — approximately 15–30% of basic-tier MCU cost per head — but the procurement decision is made by HR/general affairs teams that treat MCU as a compliance expense line item, not by the actuarial or clinical function that would evaluate the test against downstream claim cost. This creates a structural decision asymmetry: the decision-maker who controls the MCU panel (HR manager with a fixed wellness budget) has no P&L visibility into the inpatient claims generated by the undetected diabetics in the workforce; the decision-maker who bears those claims (CFO, insurer) has no authority over the MCU panel. The add-on that costs Rp 148,000–224,000 per employee could detect a condition whose inpatient complication will cost Rp 22 million to Rp 400 million — but this comparison is not made at the point of MCU procurement. This is the operational mechanism behind WH5: the incremental cost of better screening is small, but the procurement decision structure prevents the comparison from being made. Tests WH5.

**Who bears it:** Corporate employers who systematically under-invest in metabolic screening relative to the downstream savings available; employees with pre-diabetes who would benefit from early dietary and pharmacological intervention; and the employer health plan, whose loss ratio deteriorates from NCD complications that basic-tier MCU did not detect.

**Estimated cost:**
- HbA1c add-on cost for 1,000-employee MCU: Rp 148–224 million per annual cycle ($9,136–$13,827). Against: avoided inpatient complication at average Rp 22M ($1,358) per episode × estimated 4–8 preventable events per 1,000 undetected diabetics (conservative, based on international CDM evidence of 30–50% avoidable NCD hospitalisation rate applied to 86 undetected workers) = Rp 88–176 million ($5,432–$10,864) per year avoided at the minimum. The economic comparison is marginal at the individual-test level; the structural barrier is that the comparison is never made in the procurement decision, because the MCU buyer and the claims-bearer are different organizational functions. (1)(2)(4)(17)
- Indonesia's medical cost inflation at 13.6% in 2025 (GAIP, highest in Asia) is driven substantially by unmanaged NCD complications — yet employer benefit-design responses are focused on co-payment increases and utilization controls (per POJK 36/2025), not on upstream detection improvements through MCU panel enhancement. The gap between the cost lever (MCU panel) and the cost trajectory (13.6% annual medical inflation) is structurally invisible to the HR buyer because the two are managed by different functions with different reporting lines. (24)(25)
- Prodia's pricing architecture for corporate clients is not published at a per-test level, but the tiered product structure (Healthy Life I / II / III, Wellness Basic / Medium / Premium) makes the economics of panel upgrade transparent: moving from FBG-only to HbA1c-inclusive is a known upgrade cost, and corporate procurement teams can and do optimize toward the lower tier. (11)

**Willingness to pay:**

**Frequency:**
- 94% of Indonesian companies provide inpatient care benefits to employees (MMB HBS 2024, n=470 companies) — confirming that the financial exposure from inpatient NCD complications is a near-universal employer obligation; yet MCU panel completeness is not tracked as a metric in any Indonesian employer benefits benchmarking survey reviewed. (MMB HBS 2024 — https://www.marsh.com/id/en/services/employee-health-benefits/insights/id-indonesia-health-and-benefits-study.html)
- [ASSUMED-5]: What percentage of Indonesian corporate MCU procurements select the basic tier (FBG-only, no HbA1c) vs. a tier that includes HbA1c? To validate: ask Prodia corporate sales team, Kimia Farma Diagnostika, and Pramita about the tier distribution of corporate MCU orders in 2023–2024.
- Prodia's 2024 strategy notes focus on increasing the number of tests per MCU transaction and upgrading corporate clients from basic to comprehensive panels — which implies the current reality is that a significant proportion of corporate MCU transactions are at the basic tier without HbA1c. (6)
- Indonesia's working-age population with diabetes (18–59) had 1.6% diagnosed, but only 1.3% receiving treatment as prescribed (SKI 2023) — confirming that the detection-to-management funnel is broken at every stage, including at the MCU entry point. (7)

**Evidenced or assumed:**
(1) MMB Indonesia HBS 2024 — inpatient claim costs: https://www.marsh.com/id/en/services/employee-health-benefits/insights/id-indonesia-health-and-benefits-study.html
(2) IDF Diabetes Atlas 2024 — Indonesia undiagnosed rate, scale of undetected population: https://idf.org/news/idf-diabetes-atlas-11th-edition/
(4) HbA1c pricing — Rp 148,000–224,000 per test: https://reblood.com/blog/pemeriksaan-hba1c-harga-rp-148-000-khusus-pejuang-donor
(6) Prodia AR 2024 — corporate tier upgrades as strategy: https://d3ftma7dyyzt91.cloudfront.net/2025/04/07/081518/Prodia%20AR%202024.pdf
(7) Databoks / SKI 2023 — 11.7% prevalence, working-age treatment gap: https://databoks.katadata.co.id/en/consumer-services/statistics/8a95a31a9cb29b4/indonesias-diabetes-prevalence-rises-to-117-in-2023
(9) Tzu Chi MCU tiers — basic excludes HbA1c: https://tzuchihospital.co.id/article/paket-mcu-lengkap
(11) Prodia MCU package structure — tiered pricing, HbA1c at second tier: https://www.idxchannel.com/milenomic/lengkap-cek-rincian-harga-medical-check-up-prodia-prda-terbaru-2025/all
(24) Indonesia medical cost inflation authoritative series per Stage 1 DR5: MMB Health Trends 2025 (global insurer panel) — 14.6% (2023 actual) / 17.9% (2024 forecast) / 19.0% (2025 forecast). Aon GMTRR 2025/2026 (different panel) — 13.1% (2024 actual) / 16.2% (2025 forecast) / 16.9% (2026 forecast). The 13.6% figure in Indonesia trade press traces to MMB's retiree-family benchmark (Prastyo & Gani PMC12425502), a separate product. Sources: https://www.fwd.co.id/files/v3/assets/blt0c90459332411c22/bltea93d95ff78d64c5/67b8270218515989b9a6916c/Mercer_Marsh_Benefit_Health_Trend_2025_(1).pdf ; https://assets.aon.com/-/media/files/aon/reports/2025/aon-global-medical-trend-rates-report-2025.pdf ; https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12425502/
(25) POJK 36/2025 — co-payment provisions, managed care encouragement but no MCU panel mandate: https://ojk.go.id/id/regulasi/Pages/POJK-36-Tahun-2025-Penguatan-Ekosistem-Asuransi-Kesehatan.aspx; https://www.milliman.com/en/insight/indonesia-health-regulations-ealert-key-updates
(26) PMC systematic review CDM ROI — $2.83–$9.89 per $1 invested: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8582197/
(27) Indonesia productivity burden — undiagnosed NCD causes 10 lost work days/year: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4051736/

**Root Causes**

- **RC1: The MCU procurement decision is made by HR/general affairs/procurement with a fixed wellness-and-compliance budget — these functions have no visibility into health plan claims costs, so the economic argument for HbA1c add-on is never made at the point of panel selection.** Indonesian corporate organizational structures typically separate K3/occupational health compliance (reporting to HR or operations) from employee health benefits management (HR or finance, interfacing with insurer/TPA) and from financial performance reporting (CFO/finance). The MCU buyer (HR/GA) is evaluated on per-head MCU cost and compliance rate, not on subsequent claims trends. The health plan claims cost trend is monitored by a different person (HR director or finance, reviewing annual renewal quote) who typically does not review the MCU panel composition. No corporate governance mechanism routinely connects MCU panel decisions to claims cost attribution. This organizational design produces a permanent blind spot between the screening investment and the claims outcome. [ASSUMED-4 (from PP 4.2 — re-indexed here as ASSUMED-5 per PP 4.3 context)]: What is the organizational decision-making chain for MCU panel selection at large Indonesian employers (500+ employees)? To validate: interview HR directors at Astra International, Bank BRI, or PLN; ask Prodia OHI sales team who the buyer decision-makers are for MCU panel design.

- **RC2: Lab chains have no commercial incentive to recommend upgrading from basic to comprehensive panels because the basic-tier corporate MCU is a high-volume, low-friction product that generates predictable contract revenue — while HbA1c add-on conversations add sales complexity.** Prodia's corporate client revenue (IDR 354.93 billion / 2024) is generated primarily through high-volume, multi-site annual MCU contracts with major employers (Pertamina, PLN, BUMN, large private corporates). These are negotiated annually on per-head pricing with volume discounts. The incentive for Prodia's corporate sales team is contract retention and volume growth — not panel depth per employee. Adding HbA1c to every contract would require clinical education of the buyer, price renegotiation, and potentially losing a contract to a lower-priced competitor who doesn't include HbA1c. Prodia's growth strategy, per the 2024 AR, is described as outlet expansion and product-range expansion — not specifically as clinical-panel depth improvement for existing corporate clients. [ASSUMED-6]: Does Prodia's corporate sales team have a structured clinical advisory process for recommending MCU panel upgrades to corporate clients? To validate: ask Prodia OHI or Prodia corporate sales management about their panel upgrade recommendation rate and sales process.

- **RC3: There is no employer benchmarking or regulatory floor that establishes HbA1c as a standard element of corporate MCU, so employers have no normative signal that their panel is clinically suboptimal.** MMB Indonesia HBS 2024, Aon Indonesia, and WTW Indonesia benefits benchmarking surveys track inpatient/outpatient benefit prevalence, MCU provision as a benefit-category, and premium cost trends — but do not publish data on MCU panel composition or HbA1c inclusion rates. An HR director reviewing peer benchmarks sees that 80–94% of companies provide annual MCU (consistent with Permenaker compliance) but has no signal about what tests those MCUs contain. Without a normative reference for "best-practice MCU panel," employers cannot self-identify as having a suboptimal panel. The benchmark gap thus normalizes FBG-only screening as the market standard, because no data source contradicts this assumption. [ASSUMED-7]: Does any Indonesian HR or employee benefits benchmarking survey track MCU panel composition or HbA1c inclusion rates? To validate: request survey instruments from Marsh Indonesia, WTW Indonesia, Aon Indonesia benefits advisory teams.

