## Cohort scaling — 500 / 1,000 / 5,000 employee workforces (gap-fill per width critic W2)

PP 6.3 modeled the 1,000-employee reference workforce. The input scope specified 500, 1,000, AND 5,000 employee workforce sizes. The 1,000-employee model scales approximately linearly for the steady-state cost components (direct claims, productivity loss) and quadratically for low-event-rate complication costs (Poisson statistics at small n).

**Reference parameters (from PP 6.3, validated):**
- Adult T2DM prevalence applied to corporate workforce: 11.7% (SKI 2023, IDF 2024 cross-validates at 11.0% age-standardised)
- Undiagnosed rate: 73.2% (IDF 2024 canonical; SKI 2023 arithmetic implies ~81% — see contradictions-resolved.json C-A2)
- Direct cost per managed diabetic (Indonesia, Kristina 2020): USD 1,062/year
- Indirect cost per diabetic (lower bound, Kristina 2020): USD 146/year
- Indirect cost per diabetic (upper bound, Finkelstein + ADA-ratio applied): USD 400-1,100/year
- Annual cardiovascular event rate among T2DM: 1-3% (ASSUMED-4; PERKENI clinical guidelines benchmark range)
- Per-episode cost public JKN AMI: Rp 47-51M / USD 3,000-3,300 (Dwiputra 2024)
- Per-episode cost private hospital metabolic complication: Rp 100-400M / USD 6,400-25,800 (ASSUMED-3 industry knowledge)

### 500-employee workforce

- T2DM cohort: ~59 diabetics (~43 undiagnosed)
- Hypertension cohort: ~150 (assumes 30% prevalence cross-validated SKI 2023)
- Pre-diabetes cohort: ~100 (assumes 20%; ASSUMED-11)
- Direct cost (managed diabetics): ~USD 62,700/year
- Indirect cost (lower bound USD 146): ~USD 8,600/year
- Indirect cost (upper bound USD 400-1,100): ~USD 24,000-65,000/year
- Cardiovascular events (1-3% annual rate): 0-2 events × USD 6,400-25,800 = USD 0-52,000/year (Poisson-skewed at small n; expected value USD 5,500-22,000/year)
- **Implied annual cost burden range: USD 75,000-260,000/year**
- Compare to MCU procurement budget at Rp 600K × 500 = USD 19,300/year; CDM screening incremental ~USD 5/employee × 500 = USD 2,500/year

### 1,000-employee workforce (the reference; from PP 6.3)

- T2DM cohort: ~117 diabetics (~86 undiagnosed)
- Hypertension cohort: ~300; pre-diabetes ~200
- Direct cost: ~USD 124,000/year
- Indirect cost lower bound: ~USD 17,000/year; upper bound: ~USD 47,000-129,000/year
- Cardiovascular events 1-3% rate: 1-3 events × USD 6,400-25,800 = USD 6,400-78,000/year
- **Implied annual cost burden range: USD 150,000-600,000/year** (PP 6.3 published figure)
- Compare to MCU procurement: USD 38,700/year; CDM screening incremental: USD 5,000/year

### 5,000-employee workforce

- T2DM cohort: ~585 diabetics (~428 undiagnosed)
- Hypertension cohort: ~1,500; pre-diabetes ~1,000
- Direct cost: ~USD 621,000/year
- Indirect cost lower bound: ~USD 85,000/year; upper bound: ~USD 234,000-644,000/year
- Cardiovascular events 1-3% rate: 6-18 events × USD 6,400-25,800 = USD 38,000-464,000/year (Poisson less skewed at larger n; expected value USD 96,000-260,000/year)
- **Implied annual cost burden range: USD 750,000-2,800,000/year**
- Compare to MCU procurement: USD 193,000/year; CDM screening incremental: USD 25,000/year

**Note:** Linear scaling is approximate. The complication-event component does not scale linearly at the 500-employee size (Poisson statistics make rare-event-driven cost volatile at small n); the 5,000-employee size is where rare-event averaging produces more stable expected-value math. The 1,000-employee size is the methodologically cleanest reference cohort and is the primary published model.

**Caveats applicable to all three cohort sizes:**
- Corporate workforce age skew (typically 25-55) may reduce T2DM prevalence by 20-30% vs national general-adult (15+) baseline; this is a documented limitation, not corrected in the above (per critic-dialectic F8).
- Undiagnosed rate from IDF (73.2%) is general-population; urban formal-sector workers may have higher detection (employer MCU access) — but the 73.2% applies to those who have not had their condition flagged, which is the relevant cohort for CDM intervention regardless of MCU access if the MCU panel excludes HbA1c (Stages 3, 4).
- Private hospital complication cost (Rp 100-400M / USD 6,400-25,800) is ASSUMED-3 (industry knowledge, no primary source); the public JKN floor (Rp 47-51M / USD 3,000-3,300) per Dwiputra 2024 understates the corporate-channel reality where employees use private hospitals.



